Lagrange's theorem restricts element orders to $1$, $2$, $4$, and $8$. An element of order $8$ gives $C_8$, while if every non-trivial element has order $2$, repeated use of the direct product theorem gives $C_2 times C_2 times C_2$. If an element has order $4$, the possible relations with an element outside its generated subgroup split into an abelian case giving $C_4 times C_2$, a dihedral case giving $D_8$, and a case with $b^2 = a^2$ giving $Q_8$.
